! ============================================================
! 04a  Open-shell O2: <S^2> and what "converged" does not mean
! ============================================================
! Molecular oxygen has a TRIPLET ground state -- two unpaired
! electrons in the degenerate pi* pair.  It is the standard
! demonstration that a closed-shell picture of a stable
! molecule can be simply wrong.
!
! WHAT TO LOOK AT:
!   * <S^2> printed at the end.  The exact value for a pure
!     triplet is S(S+1) = 2.000.  UHF gives ~2.03 here: the
!     single determinant is contaminated by higher spin
!     states.  Below ~2.05 this is usually tolerable; much
!     above it, the wavefunction is not the state you asked
!     for and energies are not trustworthy.
!   * the bond length, ~1.16 A HF against 1.21 A experimental.
!
! NOW THE POINT OF THIS EXAMPLE.  Re-run it with
!     %uks_occupation auto
! (the default) instead of STRICT.  AUTO holds the occupation
! pattern fixed to the initial guess for the first few SCF
! iterations, which helps a difficult open-shell system keep
! its intended state -- but when the guess ordering is wrong
! it locks the SCF into the WRONG state, and the answer then
! depends on the initial guess and on any level shift.
! On O2 the two settings can differ by a full Hartree.
!
! The lesson generalizes: an SCF that "converged" has found A
! stationary point of the energy, not necessarily the LOWEST
! one.  For open-shell and charge-transfer systems, check.
! %stability check is the systematic way to do it.
%method  HF
%basis   6-31G(d,p)
%job     opt+freq
%charge  0
%mult    3
%uks_occupation strict

%geo
 O   0.0   0.0   0.00
 O   0.0   0.0   1.21
